## DESCRIPTION
## Linear Algebra
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## KEYWORDS ('linear algebra','vector space','inner product','best approximation')

## DBsubject('Linear Algebra')
## DBchapter('Inner Product Spaces')
## DBsection('Orthogonality')
## Date('Feb 2015')
## Author('J. Sylvestre')
## Institution('University of Alberta, Augustana Campus')
## TitleText1('')
## EditionText1('')
## AuthorText1('')
## Section1('')
## Problem1('')

## MATHOBJECT VERSION

DOCUMENT();        # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "PGmatrixmacros.pl",  # for mbox
#  "answerHints.pl",
);

TEXT(beginproblem());
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1;

Context("Numeric");

$t = random(2,6);
$t2 = 2*$t;
$t_sq = $t*$t;
$a = non_zero_random(-9,9);
$h_func = Formula("$a * cos(pi*x/$t2)");
$ans = Formula("3*$a*(20 - pi*pi)/(pi*pi*pi) + x^2 * 15*$a*(pi*pi - 12)/(pi*pi*pi*$t_sq)");
$naive = Formula("-$a*(x^2 - $t_sq)/$t_sq");

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
The pairing $SPACE$SPACE\( \langle f,g \rangle =  \int_{-$t}^{$t} \, f(x) g(x) \, dx \)$SPACE$SPACE defines an inner product on \( C[-$t,$t]\), the space of continuous functions defined on the interval \([-$t,$t]\).
$PAR
On this interval, the graph of $SPACE$SPACE\(\displaystyle h(x) = $h_func \)$SPACE$SPACE is similar in shape to a parabola.
$PAR
Determine the parabola \(p(x)\) that best approximates \( h(x) \) on the interval \([-$t,$t]\).
$PAR
\{ mbox(
  'ANSWER: \(p(x) =\)',
  $ans->ans_rule(30),
) \}
$PAR
$BBOLD $BITALIC Note: $EITALIC $EBOLD You can enter the word pi as part of your answer to mean the number \(\pi\). For example, enter 3/(4*pi^2) to mean \(\frac{3}{4\pi^2}\).$BR
$BBOLD $BITALIC Extra: $EITALIC $EBOLD You might find it interesting to get a computer to plot both your answer parabola and $SPACE$SPACE\( h(x) \)$SPACE$SPACE on the same set of axes. Then compare this with a plot of $SPACE$SPACE\( h(x) \)$SPACE$SPACE and the "naive" approximating parabola $SPACE$SPACE\( y = $naive \)$SPACE$SPACE on the same set of axes. (This "naive" approximating polynomial has the same \(x\)- and \(y\)-intercepts as $SPACE$SPACE\(h(x)\).)
$PAR
END_TEXT
$showHint = -1;
BEGIN_HINT
The first step is to produce an orthogonal basis for $SPACE$SPACE\(\operatorname{Span}\lbrace 1,x,x^2 \rbrace\).
END_HINT
Context()->normalStrings;
ANS($ans->with(tolerance=>.00001)->cmp);

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.